<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8" ?>
<!-- This file contains the lecture notes for the section on limits at infinity. -->
  <section xml:id="limits-at-infinity">
    <title>Limits at Infinity</title>
    <p>
      In this section, we will explore limits at infinity, which describe the behavior of a function as the input grows without bound.
    </p>
    <p>
       It may be that the limit does not exist at all! For example, if a function is defined as a ratio like the examples above, the denominator vanishes at our point of interest <m>x=a,</m>
        but unlike those examples the numerator does not vanish at <m>x=a</m> to provide cancellation, then we get an undefined expression, like <m>1/0.</m> 
    </p>
    <p> 
    In such a case, we must examine the values of the functions closely in the neighborhood of
     <m>x=a</m>. If the values ``blow up" to infinity on both sides of <m>x=a</m>,
      we may say that the limit is infinity. Similarly, if the values converge to <m>-\infty</m>
       on both sides, then we may say that the limit is <m>-\infty.</m> 
       But otherwise, all we can say is that the limit is undefined. 
    </p>

    <example xml:id="ex-infinite-limits">
  <p>
    The following examples demonstrate infinite limits and behavior near a vertical asymptote:
  </p>
  <ol marker="1">
    <li>
      <p>
        <m>\displaystyle\lim_{x \to -1} \frac{x+4}{(x+1)^2} = \infty</m>
      </p>
      <p>
        Since the numerator approaches <m>3</m> and the denominator is always positive and approaches <m>0</m>, the values are positive and become arbitrarily large near <m>x = -1</m>.
      </p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <p>
        <m>\displaystyle\lim_{x \to -1} \frac{x-2}{(x+1)^2} = -\infty</m>
      </p>
      <p>
        Since the numerator approaches <m>-3</m> and the denominator is always positive and approaches <m>0</m>, all values are negative and large in magnitude near <m>x = -1</m>.
      </p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <p>
        <m>\displaystyle\lim_{x \to -1} \frac{x-2}{x+1} = \text{undefined}</m>
      </p>
      <p>
        The values are negative and large to the right of <m>x = -1</m>, but positive and large on the left side. Because the one-sided limits do not match, the two-sided limit does not exist.
      </p>
    </li>
  </ol>
</example>



    </section>